    <h2>What critical swim speed is</h2>
    <p>Critical swim speed (CSS) is the quickest pace you can hold, in open water or in a pool, without accumulating fatigue that forces you to slow down. It behaves as a threshold: it's the number every zone is built from.</p>
    <p>It's estimated from two maximal efforts over different distances. The time difference between 400 and 200 metres describes exactly what each additional 100 metres costs you at a maximal sustainable effort — and that is your CSS. The maths is simple: the difference between the two times, divided by two, is your pace per 100 metres.</p>

    <h2>How to do the test properly</h2>
    <p>The test is worth exactly what its execution is worth. Rested, after a short but real warm-up. Swim the 400 evenly and hard, not fast-then-dying: a badly paced 400 inflates the gap between the two distances and hands you a slower CSS than the one you actually have. Between the two efforts, full recovery — several minutes, until your breathing is back to normal.</p>
    <p>If the test falls apart halfway or the pacing goes wrong, don't patch it with an estimate. Repeat it the following weekend.</p>

    <h2>Loading it into TrainingPeaks</h2>
    <p>Under <strong>Settings → Zones → Swim</strong>, enter your critical swim speed as threshold pace and choose the <strong>80/20 Swimming</strong> method. You'll get exactly the values you see here — this calculator exists so you can see them first, and understand where they come from.</p>
  </div>
</section>
